{
  "A0100": "A0100 initially pressed northward toward the opposing pair while descending and accelerating, consistent with closing for an attack. Immediately after its recorded ammunition reduction and missile appearance at step 47, it shifted into a sustained right-hand break, carrying it eastward and away from the direct approach line; this most plausibly served post-launch lateral separation and reduced exposure, although the simultaneous opposing missile activity prevents attributing the turn to a confirmed defensive reaction.",
  "A0200": "A0200 maintained a largely northbound, accelerating approach throughout the window while descending, with only modest heading adjustments, suggesting continued pressure toward the opposing formation rather than disengagement. A missile object associated with A0200 appeared later, but its first appearance and unchanged ammunition count do not establish a launch in this window; the aircraft nevertheless continued advancing while A0100 separated, plausibly sustaining the team's forward attack geometry.",
  "B0100": "B0100 continued a southbound, descending and accelerating approach toward Team A for the entire window, with no clear transition into a defensive break or disengagement. Its persistent closure while A0100 turned away is most consistent with maintaining offensive pressure and preserving an opportunity to engage the remaining forward threat, though no recorded action or ammunition change identifies a specific attack decision or target.",
  "B0200": "B0200 likewise maintained a southbound approach while descending and increasing speed, remaining committed to closing the opposing formation rather than maneuvering away. Its ammunition count decreased and an associated missile appeared at step 47, supporting a missile expenditure during the exchange; the subsequent continued approach suggests it was pressing the engagement or supporting that missile rather than immediately executing a defensive reaction, with no hit or target attribution available."
}

{
  "A0100": "A0100 begins by closing northward toward the opposing formation, then sustains a broad right-hand turn that carries it eastward while descending; after the turn is established, it largely holds the resulting course. With its own missile already in flight and both B aircraft plus their inbound missiles ahead, this appears intended to create lateral separation and reduce direct exposure while preserving speed and keeping pressure on the engagement. The later stabilized eastward leg suggests repositioning rather than continued nose-on pursuit.",
  "A0200": "A0200 continues the northward approach initially, then progressively commits to a strong rightward, northeastward maneuver with substantial descent and maintained or increasing speed. The geometry is consistent with seeking a more favorable offset against the southbound B formation while avoiding a direct collision path, possibly supporting the already-launched missile from a different angle. Its increasingly steep bank and continued turn indicate an active repositioning or defensive-attack maneuver rather than simple formation travel.",
  "B0100": "B0100 maintains a largely steady southbound advance throughout the window, gradually descending while its speed remains available, with no recorded evidence of a major evasive or lateral maneuver. The aircraft is moving toward the A formation while its own missile continues southward ahead of it, so the most plausible objective is to sustain an intercept and support the ongoing missile engagement by closing behind the weapon. The absence of a sharp course change suggests no clearly supported defensive reaction within the window.",
  "B0200": "B0200 likewise continues south toward Team A, but its descent and energy state develop more aggressively than B0100's while it remains broadly aligned with the engagement axis. Its missile is also progressing toward the A side, making continued forward pressure and maintenance of a useful intercept position the best-supported intention. The relatively straight course, despite changing pitch and speed, suggests it is preserving pursuit geometry behind the missile rather than responding to a confirmed threat or disengaging."
}

{
  "A0100": "A0100 maintains a nearly level, high-altitude eastward run while steadily increasing speed, rather than joining A0200's descending turn. This appears most plausibly intended to preserve energy and create a separated northern approach or supporting position against the southbound B formation; the aircraft remains alive and retains its recorded missile count, with no observed transition requiring a more aggressive commitment.",
  "A0200": "A0200 commits to a sustained descending turn toward the opposing formation, driving eastward across the engagement while trading altitude for turning geometry and speed. The maneuver is most consistent with an aggressive attack approach or close-pass repositioning against the converging B aircraft; the simultaneous removal of both recorded A missiles and B0200's subsequent inactivity marks an exchange, but does not establish a launch, target, or hit. A0200 then continues descending and turning after that exchange.",
  "B0100": "B0100 initially presses southward toward the A formation, but during the middle of the window transitions into a pronounced bank that changes its motion toward the southeast while continuing to lose altitude. This appears most plausibly to be a defensive break or lateral repositioning as the engagement passes through close geometry, potentially after B0200 becomes inactive; the aircraft survives and continues maneuvering as A0200 descends away, although the data do not establish whether a missile specifically triggered the turn.",
  "B0200": "B0200 becomes inactive and loses valid flight data at approximately 27.2 seconds, so no tactical intention can be inferred for its behavior during the remainder of the window."
}

{
  "A0100": "A0100 initially maintained a fast, nearly level eastward run while staying well above the opposing aircraft, then began a sustained hard left turn with a gradual descent and speed reduction. The change most plausibly represents lateral repositioning toward the evolving engagement rather than a confirmed missile-evasion response: no launch or threat event directly implicates A0100, while B0100 remained offset to its north-west and A0200 was closing the same fight from below. The turn continued through the window, establishing a new northward-facing attack or support position.",
  "A0200": "A0200 began low and descending while turning left from an eastward course, then continued across the engagement with a strong north-easterly component before making smaller heading corrections. This is most consistent with an aggressive pursuit or attack-positioning maneuver against B0100, which lay ahead and north-west initially; the aircraft's changing relative position suggests an attempt to cross into a favorable firing geometry rather than disengage. Its later leveling of the turn and sustained forward motion indicate continued pressure after the initial intercept setup.",
  "B0100": "B0100 spent the window descending while turning from a south-easterly course toward nearly east, with its motion gradually becoming less southward as A0200 approached from behind and south-west. The pattern most plausibly reflects defensive repositioning intended to preserve separation and avoid presenting a stable engagement geometry, possibly while supporting missiles already in flight; its recorded ammunition was already zero, and the surviving missile activity does not identify a target or launch within this window. It remained active and alive as the aircraft crossed the engagement area.",
  "B0200": "B0200 was inactive with invalid flight state throughout the entire recording window, so no tactical intention can be inferred from the supplied data."
}

{
  "A0100": "A0100 began in a high, descending left-hand repositioning that progressively aligned it toward the lower opposing aircraft, most plausibly to establish launch geometry rather than immediately close for a gun engagement. Its missile count fell at step 312, after which it continued the broad turn while accelerating and eventually eased the turn, consistent with a post-launch break intended to reduce exposure and reposition; the missile was removed when B0100 became inactive, although that does not independently confirm a hit.",
  "A0200": "A0200 maintained a fast approach through the opposing aircraft's vicinity, repeatedly adjusting its heading while descending only modestly, suggesting an attempt to preserve an intercept or attack position on B0100. As A0100 launched and B0100 continued moving away and climbing, A0200 kept pressing forward rather than disengaging, consistent with sustaining pressure and exploiting the remaining opponent; B0100 later became inactive, but A0200's unchanged ammunition does not establish that it fired.",
  "B0100": "B0100 entered the window with no recorded missiles remaining and an already-present missile, then continued toward the engagement while gradually turning and climbing. The later climb and changing heading are most plausibly an attempt to create separation or improve survivability as A0200 approached and A0100's missile appeared on a converging southwestward path, though the telemetry cannot establish whether this was a deliberate defensive reaction; B0100 became inactive as the A missile was removed.",
  "B0200": "B0200 was inactive throughout the entire window, with invalid state data and no recorded flight activity, so its tactical intention cannot be inferred."
}
